{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem was in `percentile`: it used `int(p / 100 * n)` as a 0-based index, which is one rank too high for nearest-rank (e.g. p50 on 1..10 gave 6 instead of 5) and could index past the end at p=100.\n\n**Fix:** Use the standard nearest-rank rule — ordinal rank `ceil(p / 100 * n)`, then take `ordered[rank - 1]`. Handle `p <= 0` and `p >= 100` explicitly so min/max match the docstring. Removed the unused `old_percentile` wrapper and the stale bug comment.\n\n`python3 -m pytest tests/visible -q` — 2 passed."}]},"session_id":"73f5f5a7-de3f-433a-9b1c-9872c946bd59"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":80020,"duration_api_ms":80020,"is_error":false,"result":"Exploring the stats module and running tests to identify issues.\n\n\nFixing nearest-rank percentile: use `ceil(p/100 * n)` and index at `rank - 1` instead of truncating with `int()`.\n\n\nThe problem was in `percentile`: it used `int(p / 100 * n)` as a 0-based index, which is one rank too high for nearest-rank (e.g. p50 on 1..10 gave 6 instead of 5) and could index past the end at p=100.\n\n**Fix:** Use the standard nearest-rank rule — ordinal rank `ceil(p / 100 * n)`, then take `ordered[rank - 1]`. Handle `p <= 0` and `p >= 100` explicitly so min/max match the docstring. Removed the unused `old_percentile` wrapper and the stale bug comment.\n\n`python3 -m pytest tests/visible -q` — 2 passed.","session_id":"73f5f5a7-de3f-433a-9b1c-9872c946bd59","request_id":"81e87a2b-78cd-42cf-8ab0-7868c7a8c05c","usage":{"inputTokens":12253,"outputTokens":1839,"cacheReadTokens":75486,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
